{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"optional-variant-any","title":"optional, variant & any","group":"Errors, Streams & Utilities","order":65,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C++17 vocabulary types: <code>std::optional&lt;T&gt;</code> for “a value or nothing”, <code>std::variant&lt;A, B, …&gt;</code> for “exactly one of these types”, <code>std::any</code> for “any type at all”.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-dashed\"></i><code>std::optional</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;map&gt;\n#include &lt;optional&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nstd::optional&lt;int&gt; findAge(const std::map&lt;std::string, int&gt;&amp; db, const std::string&amp; name) {\n    auto it = db.find(name);\n    if (it == db.end()) return std::nullopt;          // “no value” — no sentinel like -1\n    return it-&gt;second;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::map&lt;std::string, int&gt; db{{\"ada\", 36}};\n    if (auto age = findAge(db, \"ada\")) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"ada \" &lt;&lt; *age &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // contextual bool\n    auto missing = findAge(db, \"bob\");\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; missing.has_value() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; missing.value_or(-1) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    try { std::cout &lt;&lt; missing.value(); }                                       // throws if empty\n    catch (const std::bad_optional_access&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"bad_optional_access\\n\"; }\n\n    std::optional&lt;std::string&gt; nick;\n    nick = \"Addy\";\n    nick.reset();\n    nick.emplace(3, 'z');\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *nick &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; nick-&gt;size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ada 36\n0 -1\nbad_optional_access\nzzz 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>optional</code> stores the value <em>inline</em> (no heap allocation) plus a <code>bool</code>. Use it for return values that may be absent and for optional members; don't use <code>optional&lt;T&amp;&gt;</code> (not allowed) — use <code>T*</code> for optional references.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch-3\"></i><code>std::variant</code> — a type-safe union</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;variant&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;class... Fs&gt; struct overloaded : Fs... { using Fs::operator()...; };   // visitor helper\n\nusing Json = std::variant&lt;std::nullptr_t, bool, int, std::string&gt;;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;Json&gt; values{nullptr, true, 42, std::string(\"hi\")};\n    for (const auto&amp; v : values) {\n        std::visit(overloaded{\n            [](std::nullptr_t)          { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"null\\n\"; },\n            [](bool b)                  { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"bool \" &lt;&lt; b &lt;&lt; '\\n'; },\n            [](int i)                   { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"int \" &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; '\\n'; },\n            [](const std::string&amp; s)    { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"string \" &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; '\\n'; },\n        }, v);\n    }\n\n    std::variant&lt;int, std::string&gt; x = 5;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x.index() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::holds_alternative&lt;int&gt;(x) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::get&lt;int&gt;(x) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    x = \"now a string\";\n    if (auto* s = std::get_if&lt;std::string&gt;(&amp;x)) std::cout &lt;&lt; *s &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    try { std::get&lt;int&gt;(x); } catch (const std::bad_variant_access&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"bad_variant_access\\n\"; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>null\nbool 1\nint 42\nstring hi\n0 1 5\nnow a string\nbad_variant_access</code></pre></figure>\n<p>A <code>variant</code> holds exactly one alternative at a time, knows which (<code>index()</code>), and its size is the largest alternative plus a tag. It's the value-semantic alternative to a class hierarchy when the set of types is closed: no heap, no virtual calls, and <code>std::visit</code> forces you to handle every case.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i><code>std::any</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;any&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::any a = 42;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.type().name() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::any_cast&lt;int&gt;(a) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    a = std::string(\"text\");\n    if (auto* s = std::any_cast&lt;std::string&gt;(&amp;a)) std::cout &lt;&lt; *s &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // pointer form: nullptr on mismatch\n    try { std::any_cast&lt;double&gt;(a); } catch (const std::bad_any_cast&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"bad_any_cast\\n\"; }\n    a.reset();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.has_value() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>i 42\ntext\nbad_any_cast\n0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Holds</th><th>Checked by</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>optional&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>A <code>T</code> or nothing</td><td><code>has_value()</code>, <code>if (opt)</code></td><td>Maybe-absent results</td></tr><tr><td><code>variant&lt;Ts...&gt;</code></td><td>Exactly one of a <em>closed</em> set of types</td><td><code>index()</code>, <code>holds_alternative</code>, <code>visit</code></td><td>State machines, AST nodes, message types, parse results</td></tr><tr><td><code>any</code></td><td>Any copyable type</td><td><code>any_cast</code> (run time)</td><td>Rarely: plugin/config bags with unknown types</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">C++23</div><p>C++23 adds <code>std::expected&lt;T, E&gt;</code> — a value <strong>or an error</strong> — for error handling without exceptions. (libc++ in C++20 mode doesn't provide it; with <code>-std=c++23</code> it's available.)</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you use std::variant instead of inheritance?</summary><div><p>When the set of alternatives is fixed and known (closed), you want value semantics (copyable, no heap allocation), and you're adding <em>operations</em> more often than <em>types</em>. Inheritance suits open sets of types where new subclasses get added and operations are fixed.</p></div></details></section>"}